 2. Musk sets sights on U.S. wireless oligarchy 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photos: Win McNamee and Eric Thayer/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Elon Musk is looking to shake up America's wireless industry, potentially bringing the first major new competitive threat in years to a business dominated by three companies.

 Why it matters: The cellular industry has seen enormous technological change but relatively little disruption to its competitive structure. Musk has the money, spectrum and satellite network to attempt something different — though he faces daunting obstacles.

 Driving the news: SpaceX is assembling the pieces to become a major U.S. wireless carrier, potentially building its own terrestrial network and buying available spectrum. 

 The moves are meant either to build the capability to bypass existing carriers or to bring them to the table for a deal that would allow Musk to strike a deal for wholesale access to their networks. Musk may have reason to move quickly while the current administration and its regulatory appointees remain in place, a reason some analysts have cited in the context of proposals for a merger between Tesla and SpaceX . What they're saying: Blair Levin, a former Federal Communications Commission chief of staff who now heads policy at Optimal Advisory, says Musk's financial resources distinguish him from previous challengers.

 "For the first time, there is an entity capable of scaring investors in the traditional big three," Levin told Axios. Keep reading .

 
 
